Yes, it is possible to use C# to analyze the nutritional value of a list of fruits and vegetables and determine which ones are the most nutrient-dense. To do this, you would need to obtain nutritional data for each item on the list and then use that data to calculate a nutrient density score for each item.
Here is some sample code that can help with this analysis:
```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Linq;
class Program
{
    static void Main(string[] args)
    {
        // Define a list of fruits and vegetables
        List<string> items = new List<string> { "kale", "blueberries", "spinach", "avocado", "sweet potato" };
        // Define a dictionary to store the nutritional data for each item
        Dictionary<string, Dictionary<string, double>> nutritionData = new Dictionary<string, Dictionary<string, double>>();
        // Add nutritional data for each item to the dictionary
        nutritionData.Add("kale", new Dictionary<string, double> { { "vitamin A", 206.7 }, { "vitamin C", 134.4 }, { "iron", 1.5 }, { "calcium", 101.0 }, { "antioxidants", 3.7 } });
        nutritionData.Add("blueberries", new Dictionary<string, double> { { "vitamin A", 80.0 }, { "vitamin C", 14.0 }, { "iron", 0.4 }, { "calcium", 9.0 }, { "antioxidants", 13.2 } });
        nutritionData.Add("spinach", new Dictionary<string, double> { { "vitamin A", 2813.0 }, { "vitamin C", 28.1 }, { "iron", 2.7 }, { "calcium", 99.0 }, { "antioxidants", 2.9 } });
        nutritionData.Add("avocado", new Dictionary<string, double> { { "vitamin A", 146.0 }, { "vitamin C", 10.0 }, { "iron", 0.6 }, { "calcium", 12.0 }, { "antioxidants", 2.9 } });
        nutritionData.Add("sweet potato", new Dictionary<string, double> { { "vitamin A", 19218.0 }, { "vitamin C", 2.4 }, { "iron", 0.7 }, { "calcium", 30.0 }, { "antioxidants", 2.5 } });
        // Calculate the nutrient density score for each item
        Dictionary<string, double> nutrientDensityScores = new Dictionary<string, double>();
        foreach (string item in items)
        {
            double nutrientDensityScore = 0;
            foreach (KeyValuePair<string, double> nutrient in nutritionData[item])
            {
                nutrientDensityScore += nutrient.Value;
            }
            nutrientDensityScores[item] = nutrientDensityScore;
        }
        // Sort the items by their nutrient density scores
        var sortedItems = from item in nutrientDensityScores
                          orderby item.Value descending
                          select item;
        // Print out the sorted list of items and their nutrient density scores
        Console.WriteLine("Nutrient density scores:");
        foreach (KeyValuePair<string, double> item in sortedItems)
        {
            Console.WriteLine(item.Key + ": " + item.Value);
        }
    }
}
```
This code defines a list of fruits and vegetables, as well as a dictionary to store the nutritional data for each item. It then calculates the nutrient density score for each item by adding up the values for each nutrient in the item's nutritional data. Finally, it sorts the items by their nutrient density scores and prints out the sorted list.

