Sure, here's a Java implementation of the quicksort algorithm that meets all the requirements you've specified:
```java
import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.Random;
public class QuickSort {
    private static final int INSERTION_SORT_THRESHOLD = 47; // Tuned for performance
    public static void quicksort(int[] arr) {
        quicksort(arr, 0, arr.length - 1);
    }
    private static void quicksort(int[] arr, int left, int right) {
        if (right - left < INSERTION_SORT_THRESHOLD) {
            insertionSort(arr, left, right);
            return;
        }
        int pivotIndex = choosePivotIndex(arr, left, right);
        int pivotNewIndex = partition(arr, left, right, pivotIndex);
        quicksort(arr, left, pivotNewIndex - 1);
        quicksort(arr, pivotNewIndex + 1, right);
    }
    private static int choosePivotIndex(int[] arr, int left, int right) {
        // Choose a random pivot index between left and right
        Random rand = new Random();
        return rand.nextInt(right - left + 1) + left;
    }
    private static int partition(int[] arr, int left, int right, int pivotIndex) {
        int pivotValue = arr[pivotIndex];
        swap(arr, pivotIndex, right); // Move pivot to end
        int storeIndex = left;
        for (int i = left; i < right; i++) {
            if (arr[i] < pivotValue) {
                swap(arr, i, storeIndex);
                storeIndex++;
            }
        }
        swap(arr, storeIndex, right); // Move pivot to its final place
        return storeIndex;
    }
    private static void insertionSort(int[] arr, int left, int right) {
        for (int i = left + 1; i <= right; i++) {
            int key = arr[i];
            int j = i - 1;
            while (j >= left && arr[j] > key) {
                arr[j + 1] = arr[j];
                j--;
            }
            arr[j + 1] = key;
        }
    }
    private static void swap(int[] arr, int i, int j) {
        int temp = arr[i];
        arr[i] = arr[j];
        arr[j] = temp;
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] arr = { 5, 3, 7, 2, 8, 4, 1, 6 };
        System.out.println("Before sorting: " + Arrays.toString(arr));
        quicksort(arr);
        System.out.println("After sorting: " + Arrays.toString(arr));
    }
}
```
Here's an explanation of how the code works:
- The `quicksort` method is the main entry point for the algorithm. It simply calls the private `quicksort` method with the full range of the array.
- The private `quicksort` method is where the actual sorting happens. If the range of the array is small enough (less than `INSERTION_SORT_THRESHOLD` elements), it uses an insertion sort instead of quicksort to improve performance. Otherwise, it chooses a pivot index, partitions the array around the pivot, and recursively sorts the two partitions.
- The `choosePivotIndex` method simply chooses a random pivot index between `left` and `right`.
- The `partition` method partitions the array around the pivot value. It first moves the pivot to the end of the range, then iterates through the range and swaps elements to put all elements less than the pivot to the left of the pivot, and all elements greater than or equal to the pivot to the right of the pivot. Finally, it moves the pivot to its final position in the middle of the range and returns its index.
- The `insertionSort` method is used when the range of the array is small enough. It performs an insertion sort on the range between `left` and `right`.
- The `swap` method is a simple utility method that swaps two elements in the array.
- The `main` method is just a simple test program that sorts an example array and prints the before and after arrays.
This implementation has an average time complexity of O(nlog(n)) and minimizes the amount of extra space used by using in-place sorting. It can handle arrays with duplicate values, and is tuned for performance with a small threshold for when to switch to insertion sort. It also takes into account the possibility of the array being extremely large, as well as the presence of outliers and extreme values in the array, by using a random pivot selection strategy.

