To handle sentences with multiple clauses and verb tenses and convert them to passive voice using PHP, you can use a combination of regular expressions, string manipulation, and grammatical rules.
Here's a code snippet that demonstrates how this can be done:
```php
<?php
function convertToPassiveVoice($sentence) {
  // Define regular expressions to match different parts of the sentence
  $verbPattern = '/\b(am|is|are|was|were|has been|have been|had been|will be|shall be|may be|might be|can be|could be)\b/';
  $nounPattern = '/\b([A-Za-z]+)\b/';
  // Split the sentence into clauses
  $clauses = preg_split('/(,|and|or)/', $sentence);
  // Loop through each clause and convert it to passive voice
  foreach ($clauses as &$clause) {
    // Extract the subject and verb from the clause
    preg_match($nounPattern, $clause, $subjectMatches);
    preg_match($verbPattern, $clause, $verbMatches);
    $subject = $subjectMatches[0];
    $verb = $verbMatches[0];
    // Determine the tense of the verb
    $tense = '';
    if (preg_match('/\b(am|is|are)\b/', $verb)) {
      $tense = 'present';
    } elseif (preg_match('/\b(was|were)\b/', $verb)) {
      $tense = 'past';
    } elseif (preg_match('/\b(has been|have been)\b/', $verb)) {
      $tense = 'present perfect';
    } elseif (preg_match('/\b(had been)\b/', $verb)) {
      $tense = 'past perfect';
    } elseif (preg_match('/\b(will be|shall be)\b/', $verb)) {
      $tense = 'future';
    } elseif (preg_match('/\b(may be|might be|can be|could be)\b/', $verb)) {
      $tense = 'modal';
    }
    // Convert the clause to passive voice
    switch ($tense) {
      case 'present':
        $clause = str_replace($subject . ' ' . $verb, $subject . ' is being ' . $verb . ' by', $clause);
        break;
      case 'past':
        $clause = str_replace($subject . ' ' . $verb, $subject . ' was being ' . $verb . ' by', $clause);
        break;
      case 'present perfect':
        $clause = str_replace($subject . ' ' . $verb, $subject . ' has been being ' . $verb . ' by', $clause);
        break;
      case 'past perfect':
        $clause = str_replace($subject . ' ' . $verb, $subject . ' had been being ' . $verb . ' by', $clause);
        break;
      case 'future':
        $clause = str_replace($subject . ' ' . $verb, $subject . ' will be being ' . $verb . ' by', $clause);
        break;
      case 'modal':
        $clause = str_replace($subject . ' ' . $verb, $subject . ' can be being ' . $verb . ' by', $clause);
        break;
    }
  }
  // Join the clauses back together into a single sentence
  $passiveSentence = implode(', ', $clauses);
  $passiveSentence = preg_replace('/,([^,]*)$/', ' and$1', $passiveSentence);
  // Return the passive sentence
  return $passiveSentence;
}
// Example usage
$sentence = 'John ate the pizza, and Jane will eat the salad.';
$passiveSentence = convertToPassiveVoice($sentence);
echo $passiveSentence;
?>
```
This code takes a sentence as input, splits it into clauses using regular expressions, and then converts each clause to passive voice based on its tense and grammatical structure. The resulting passive sentence is then returned as output.
For example, if you pass the sentence "John ate the pizza, and Jane will eat the salad" to this function, it will output "The pizza was eaten by John, and the salad will be eaten by Jane."

