What are the distinctive characteristics and recurring patterns of prime numbers ranging from 50 to 70? Are there any exceptions or anomalies worth mentioning?
Furthermore, could you please provide the Scala code that generates a table of prime numbers within this range, along with their respective factors and multiples?
```scala
import scala.collection.mutable.ListBuffer
import scala.math._
def isPrime(n: Int): Boolean = {
  if (n <= 1) false
  else if (n == 2) true
  else !(2 to (sqrt(n).toInt + 1)).exists(i => n % i == 0)
}
val primes = ListBuffer[Int]()
for (i <- 50 to 70) {
  if (isPrime(i)) primes += i
}
val table = ListBuffer[ListBuffer[Any]]()
for (prime <- primes) {
  val factors = ListBuffer[Int]()
  val multiples = ListBuffer[Int]()
  for (i <- 1 to prime) {
    if (prime % i == 0) factors += i
    multiples += i * prime
  }
  table += ListBuffer(prime, factors.toList, multiples.toList)
}
// Print table
println("| Prime | Factors | Multiples |")
println("|-------|---------|-----------|")
for (row <- table) {
  println(s"| ${row(0)} | ${row(1)} | ${row(2)} |")
}
```
This code generates a table listing the prime numbers between 50 and 70, along with their corresponding factors and multiples. It checks whether a given number is prime by dividing it by all integers from 2 up to the square root of the number, and generates the necessary data for each prime number.
In terms of properties and patterns, we can observe that there are four prime numbers within this range: 53, 59, 61, and 67. These prime numbers are all greater than 50 (the lower bound of the range), and are also all of the form 6k±1. This is a pattern that holds for many prime numbers, but there are exceptions (e.g. 2 and 3). Additionally, there are no prime numbers in this range that are multiples of each other.

